@page "/about"
@using MausritterTools.Core.Data
@using MausritterTools.Web.Components
@using MausritterTools.Web.Services
@inject GameDataProvider DataProvider

<PageTitle>About — Mausritter Tools</PageTitle>

<div class="settlement-tool">
    <h1>About</h1>

    <p class="lead">
        Mausritter Tools is a small set of browser-based helpers for
        <a href="https://mausritter.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mausritter</a>, the
        tabletop roleplaying game of brave mice by Isaac Williams.
    </p>

    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p>
        The whole app is a static site that runs in your browser. There is no server, no account and
        no analytics, and nothing you generate is uploaded anywhere. A settlement is produced purely
        from its seed, so the same link always rebuilds the same settlement.
    </p>
    <p>
        Locking a value freezes it in place so that re-rolling everything else leaves it alone.
        Editing a value locks it too. Because locks and hand edits would make a link unwieldy, the
        address bar carries only the seed and the generator settings; the full state travels through
        the JSON export, and is kept in your browser's local storage between visits.
    </p>

    <h2>What is official, and what is not</h2>
    <p>
        The settlement tables, the tavern table, the non-player mice tables, gear prices, hirelings
        and spells are all taken from the Mausritter SRD, which is published under CC BY 4.0. They
        are imported directly from the SRD source rather than retyped, so the wording is the
        author's own.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Mausritter has no shops or services table.</strong> The SRD says only that taverns
        appear in hamlets and larger, that warbands can be recruited in a town or city, that
        human-made goods are found near human populations, that a bank charges 1% to retrieve what
        it holds, that repairs cost 10% of an item's price per usage dot, and that
        &ldquo;the size of the settlement determines what types of hireling are available&rdquo;
        without ever saying how. Everything else about shops in this tool &mdash; which services
        exist, how size gates them, their stock, quirks and names &mdash; is
        <strong>an unofficial house rule original to this project</strong>. Each shop shows the SRD
        rule it was extrapolated from, so you can see where the line falls.
    </p>
    <p>
        Mouse names are also original. The SRD contains no name tables, and the lists used by the
        official generator are not published under a licence that permits reuse.
    </p>

    @if (_provenance is { Count: > 0 })
    {
        <h2>Data sources</h2>
        <table class="table table-sm">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Content</th>
                    <th>Source</th>
                    <th>Licence</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (DataProvenance source in _provenance)
                {
                    <tr>
                        <td>@source.Describes</td>
                        <td>
                            @if (source.IsSrdDerived)
                            {
                                <a href="@source.Url" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@source.Work</a>
                                @(source.Version is { Length: > 0 } v ? $" v{v}" : "")
                            }
                            else
                            {
                                <text>Original to this project</text>
                            }
                        </td>
                        <td>@(source.Licence ?? "Original work")</td>
                    </tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    }

    <h2>Source</h2>
    <p>
        The code, the imported tables and the importer script are on
        <a href="https://github.com/Structed/mausritter-tools" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Support Mausritter</h2>
    <p>
        This tool is no substitute for the game. Mausritter is pay-what-you-want in digital form and
        well worth owning: <a href="https://mausritter.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">mausritter.com</a>.
    </p>

    <LicenceFooter />
</div>

@code {
    private IReadOnlyList<DataProvenance>? _provenance;

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            GameData data = await DataProvider.GetDataAsync();
            _provenance = data.Provenance;
        }
        catch (GameDataException)
        {
            // The attribution above is static, so the page is still worth showing without the
            // per-file table.
            _provenance = null;
        }
    }
}
